/**
@mainpage Documentation of DEM-PBS
@tableofcontents

This is the Discrete Element Method (DEM) code of the class Particle-based
Simulations. Spherical particles feel gravity and linear soft-sphere contact
forces with friction (the Cundall-Strack model). The box is periodic in x and y,
with walls in z; more walls are added as functions (see walls.c). Units are SI
throughout: m, kg, s.

@section assign Assignment A4: silo discharge and the Beverloo law
The A4 notebook is the assignment text. Every piece of code you write already
exists as a function that compiles and is called from the right place: the
<a href="todo.html">todo list</a> shows them all.

Choose the run with the macro `RUN` at the top of setparameters.c, or on the
command line; the other macros there (`NUM_TEST`, `V_IMPACT`, `DT_FRACTION`,
`TILT_RATE`, `D_ORIFICE`, `T_DISCHARGE`) work the same way:

    gcc -O3 -DRUN=1 -DNUM_TEST=2 *.c -o dem -lm     Part A, two particles head-on
    gcc -O3 -DRUN=2 *.c -o dem -lm                  Part B, the tilted bed
    gcc -O3 -DRUN=3 -DD_ORIFICE=8 *.c -o dem -lm    Parts C and D, the silo
    ./dem > run.dat

| Part | Run | Where you write code |
|---|---|---|
| A, restitution | `RUN_COLLISION` (1) | initialise_collision in initialise.c |
| B, slope stability | `RUN_SLOPE` (2) | update_parameters in setparameters.c |
| C, the silo | `RUN_SILO` (3) | cylinder_wall and silo_bottom_wall in walls.c, initialise_positions_silo in initialise.c, update_parameters in setparameters.c |
| D, discharge | `RUN_SILO` (3) | count_in_silo in walls.c |

@section output Output
- The table on the screen: step, time, coordination number Z, potential,
  kinetic and total energy, and N_silo, the number of particles in the silo.
  Redirect it to a file and load that in the notebook.
- `trajectories.xyz`: a frame every `num_dt_traj` steps, in extended-XYZ format
  (position, radius and speed per particle; the box and the time in the second
  line of every frame). OVITO reads it as it is: colour by the `speed` property.

@section flow Simulation flow
1. set_parameters -> alloc_memory -> initialise
2. build_nbrlist and update_colllist, calculate_forces
3. Velocity-Verlet loop: update_parameters (time-dependent settings), half-step
   velocities, positions, update of the tangential contact displacements and of
   the lists, forces, second half-step velocities, output.

@section notes Implementation notes
- The essential difference from molecular dynamics is that a contact has *memory*:
  a sticking contact remembers the tangential displacement it has built up, which
  is what the tangential contact spring acts on. Each history is kept where the
  contact lives (beside the pair in the neighbor list for particle-particle
  contacts, in a fixed per-particle slot for particle-wall contacts) and is
  reset to zero when the contact ends.
- A wall is a function: it returns whether a particle overlaps it, the vector
  riw *from the closest point on the wall to the particle centre* (with the
  squared length set), and the local wall velocity. It is registered in
  p_parameters->wall_function[] and counted in num_walls.
- The spring and dashpot constants are derived in set_parameters from the
  restitution coefficients and the contact time; the time step is a fraction
  `DT_FRACTION` of that contact time.
- A restart facility exists (save_restart, load_restart) but the assignment does
  not need it: every run settles from the same seed, so the state at t_tilt or
  t_open is the same in every run. A restart would also lose the tangential
  contact histories, which the restart file does not store.
*/
